+### Shared browser sessions and Browserbase
+
+`effect-browser` owns modeled browser sessions, typed initialization, bounded capture and page
+control. Its `chromium` subpath launches self-managed Chromium or borrows a loopback connection;
+importing the neutral root does not load Chromium process management. Playwright 1.63 is an
+optional peer, resolved when a native connection is requested.
+
+`effect-browserbase` supplies provider resources and hosted integration for that runtime.
+`effect-agent-browser` provides `Tools.run(session, program)` over either browser source, with
+handler provisioning and supervision of browser and host callback failures. `fromSession(session)`
+adapts that same owner to `InteractiveBrowser` when needed. The default Toolkit, optional pointer
+Toolkit and separately optional keyboard Toolkit share the connection, action budget and capture
+reservation. Provider credentials, connection addresses and Live View controls remain host-only.
+
+Browserbase currently supports only a trusted-host `Unrestricted` network policy in this adapter.
+`ExactHosts` fails before allocation because Browserbase `allowedDomains` does not prove exact-host
+coverage for redirects, frames, subresources, popups and service workers. `PublicWeb` also fails
+before allocation because request interception cannot establish connection-time public-address
+containment. Neither mode silently falls back to `Unrestricted`.
+
+Provider `recordings`, `replays` and `downloads` belong to `effect-browserbase`. MP4 assembly and
+replay retrieval can outlive the interactive Scope; signed media URLs are temporary access material
+rather than artifact identity. Live `capture` belongs to `effect-browser`: it borrows an owned page
+and streams bounded JPEG frames with source/receipt clocks and drop accounting. It owns no encoder
+or filesystem and does not claim website-audio capture. The Agent adapter exports only its root,
+`adapter` and `tools`; it does not reexport generic or provider resources.
+
+Use `Browser.scoped(Chromium.launch(policy, { bootstrap }), use)` or the same supervisor with
+`BrowserbaseBrowser.open`. Static accessors use the configured services. `Browser.scoped` retains
+typed errors, joins callback resources before checked browser cleanup and checks closure on every
+exit. `Bootstrap.binding` checks the native caller's origin and document identity before decoding,
+before the handler and before replying; page-visible rejections contain no host causes or stacks.
+`Capture.stream(session, options)` owns an interval for each subscription; `Capture.start` exposes
+explicit metadata and stop controls. Neither acquires a second browser or another connection.
+
+Host handoff pauses automation before returning temporary Live View material. Resume requires an
+explicit operator-release decision and obtains a fresh observation while retaining the mutation
+permit. Keep-alive reconnect creates a new handle generation and never replays a pending mutation.
+Persistent writers require an application-owned exclusive context lease. Borrowed Chromium closure
+disconnects its owned connection without terminating the external browser process.
+
